-- March8's core vocabulary, in the explicit form (docs/MACHINE.md).
--
-- A dot at the end of a word applies it: `def.` is `def .`.
--
-- A definition is data: a quotation, a name, and `def` applied. A bracket
-- first in the quotation is the clause's context: the types of its inputs,
-- and after `--` the outputs it promises. Clauses of one name are a family;
-- applying it chooses the clause whose inputs match best
-- (doc/design/TYPES.md 2.8, 2.15).

-- How a literal becomes each type. A clause under a type's name is a
-- conversion: applying the type to a literal converts it, and a clause
-- that wants the type converts a literal it is given (TYPES.md 2.10).

[ < int# -- i64 > int#>i64. ] i64 def.
[ < int# -- f64 > int#>f64. ] f64 def.
[ < dec# -- f64 > dec#>f64. ] f64 def.
[ < int# -- money > int#>money. ] money def.
[ < dec# -- money > dec#>money. ] money def.
[ < int# -- dec# > int#>dec#. ] dec# def.
[ < int# -- bool > int#>bool. ] bool def.

-- Truth (Thomas, 2026-10-09): `bool` is 0 and 1, Iverson's brackets, and so
-- an i64 too: `mask + reduce.` counts. Comparisons leave one, and guards
-- take one. `not`, `and` and `or` are 1 - x, the minimum and the maximum,
-- which on values from 0 to 1 are also Zadeh's fuzzy connectives.

[ 1 bool. ] true def.
[ 0 bool. ] false def.
[ true. ] ⊤ def.
[ false. ] ⊥ def.

-- Classes: a union in a pattern matches any of its types, a little less
-- well than one of them alone (TYPES.md 3.6). `def` on a type names it.

i64 f64 or. money or. int# or. dec# or. num def.

-- Arithmetic. Two literals fold exactly, at compile time, and stay
-- literals; a literal beside a number takes its type. Money is added and
-- subtracted, not multiplied.

[ < int# int# -- int# > int#+. ] + def.
[ < dec# dec# -- dec# > dec#+. ] + def.
[ < i64 i64 -- i64 > i64+. ] + def.
[ < f64 f64 -- f64 > f64+. ] + def.
[ < money money -- money > money+. ] + def.

-- `-` is one value's negative, and `-+` subtracts, negating and adding in
-- one word until the compiler fuses the two (Thomas, 2026-10-09): `a b -+`
-- is a + (-b). So `-` has one meaning, as in APL.

[ < int# int# -- int# > int#-. ] -+ def.
[ < dec# dec# -- dec# > dec#-. ] -+ def.
[ < i64 i64 -- i64 > i64-. ] -+ def.
[ < f64 f64 -- f64 > f64-. ] -+ def.
[ < money money -- money > money-. ] -+ def.

[ < int# -- int# > 0 swap. -+. ] - def.
[ < dec# -- dec# > 0 swap. -+. ] - def.
[ < i64 -- i64 > 0 swap. -+. ] - def.
[ < f64 -- f64 > -1.0 f64*. ] - def.
[ < money -- money > 0 swap. -+. ] - def.
[ < a ary > [ -. ] map. ] - def.

[ < int# int# -- int# > int#*. ] * def.
[ < dec# dec# -- dec# > dec#*. ] * def.
[ < i64 i64 -- i64 > i64*. ] * def.
[ < f64 f64 -- f64 > f64*. ] * def.

-- Arithmetic lifts over arrays: a tensor and an atom, a number, at any
-- rank, or a tensor and a row one rank lower, element by element along the
-- leading axis, recursively, so the lower broadcasts across the higher's
-- trailing axes (TYPES.md 2.8; Thomas, 2026-10-09: they are all tensors).
-- `atom` matches any type that is not a container. The other value is read
-- below each element; a literal takes the elements' type.

[ < a ary atom > swap. [ over. +. ] map. swap. drop. ] + def.
[ < atom a ary > [ over. +. ] map. swap. drop. ] + def.
[ < a ary a > swap. [ over. +. ] map. swap. drop. ] + def.
[ < a a ary > [ over. +. ] map. swap. drop. ] + def.

[ < a ary atom > swap. [ over. -+. ] map. swap. drop. ] -+ def.
[ < atom a ary > [ over. swap. -+. ] map. swap. drop. ] -+ def.
[ < a ary a > swap. [ over. -+. ] map. swap. drop. ] -+ def.
[ < a a ary > [ over. swap. -+. ] map. swap. drop. ] -+ def.

[ < a ary atom > swap. [ over. *. ] map. swap. drop. ] * def.
[ < atom a ary > [ over. *. ] map. swap. drop. ] * def.
[ < a ary a > swap. [ over. *. ] map. swap. drop. ] * def.
[ < a a ary > [ over. *. ] map. swap. drop. ] * def.

[ < a ary atom > swap. [ over. div. ] map. swap. drop. ] div def.
[ < atom a ary > [ over. swap. div. ] map. swap. drop. ] div def.
[ < a ary a > swap. [ over. div. ] map. swap. drop. ] div def.
[ < a a ary > [ over. swap. div. ] map. swap. drop. ] div def.

[ < a ary atom > swap. [ over. /*. ] map. swap. drop. ] /* def.
[ < atom a ary > [ over. swap. /*. ] map. swap. drop. ] /* def.
[ < a ary a > swap. [ over. /*. ] map. swap. drop. ] /* def.
[ < a a ary > [ over. swap. /*. ] map. swap. drop. ] /* def.

-- Two arrays, element by element (Thomas, 2026-10-09: as everything else
-- is), as long as each other: checked at compile time when both lengths are
-- known. `zip` pairs elements for any word.

[ < a ary a ary > [ +. ] zip. ] + def.
[ < a ary a ary > [ -+. ] zip. ] -+ def.
[ < a ary a ary > [ *. ] zip. ] * def.

-- `xs ys q table`: q on every pair, a row for each element of xs. `×` is the
-- outer product, the tensor product: each element of A times all of B, so
-- ranks add.

[ < atom b > *. ] × def.
[ < a ary b > swap. [ over. ×. ] map. swap. drop. ] × def.

-- `dot` contracts A's last index with B's first: vector · vector is a
-- number, matrix · vector a vector, matrix · matrix the matrix product.
-- `zip` checks that the contracted lengths agree, at compile time when both
-- are known. `transpose` swaps a matrix's two indices.

[ < a ary b ary > [ *. ] zip. + reduce. ] dot def.
[ < a ary ary b ary > swap. [ over. dot. ] map. swap. drop. ] dot def.
[ < a ary ary > dup. 1 at. length. range. [ over. [ over. at. ] map. swap. drop. ] map. swap. drop. ] transpose def.

-- Multiplying is written `*`, which is typed; `⋅`, the dot operator, is its
-- glyph, as in algebra, and `×` is kept for the outer product (Thomas,
-- 2026-10-09: mathematics first, but `\cdot` at every product is too much).

[ *. ] ⋅ def.

-- Division. `/` is one over a value, its reciprocal, and `/*` divides, the
-- reciprocal and the product in one word until the compiler fuses the two
-- (Thomas, 2026-10-09): `a b /*` is a ⋅ 1/b, as `-` and `-+` are. `÷` is
-- `/`'s glyph. An integer has no reciprocal among the integers: its
-- division is `divmod`, floored, the quotient rounded down and the
-- remainder with the divisor's sign, so that a = q b + r and `-7 3
-- divmod.` is -3 2. `div` and `mod` take one of them, so the three always
-- agree; `div` of floats is their quotient. Two literals divide exactly.

[ < int# int# -- int# int# > int#-divmod. ] divmod def.
[ < i64 i64 -- i64 i64 > i64-divmod. ] divmod def.
[ < int# int# -- int# > divmod. drop. ] div def.
[ < i64 i64 -- i64 > divmod. drop. ] div def.
[ < f64 f64 -- f64 > f64/. ] div def.
[ < int# int# -- int# > divmod. swap. drop. ] mod def.
[ < i64 i64 -- i64 > divmod. swap. drop. ] mod def.
[ < a ary a ary > [ div. ] zip. ] div def.

[ < f64 -- f64 > 1.0 swap. f64/. ] / def.
[ < a ary > [ /. ] map. ] / def.
[ < f64 f64 -- f64 > f64/. ] /* def.
[ < a ary a ary > [ /*. ] zip. ] /* def.
[ /. ] ÷ def.

-- Comparisons leave a bool.

[ < i64 i64 -- bool > i64lt?. ] lt? def.
[ < f64 f64 -- bool > f64lt?. ] lt? def.
[ < money money -- bool > money-lt?. ] lt? def.
[ < i64 i64 -- bool > swap. i64lt?. ] gt? def.
[ < f64 f64 -- bool > swap. f64lt?. ] gt? def.
[ < money money -- bool > swap. money-lt?. ] gt? def.
[ < i64 i64 -- bool > i64eq?. ] eq? def.
[ < f64 f64 -- bool > f64eq?. ] eq? def.
[ < money money -- bool > money-eq?. ] eq? def.
[ < string string -- bool > string-eq?. ] eq? def.

-- At most, at least, not equal: `≤`, `≥` and `≠`. "Less, or equal" rather
-- than "not greater", so that a float that is no number, NaN, is at most
-- nothing and at least nothing, but not equal to anything, itself included.

[ < a a > over. over. lt?. rot. rot. eq?. or. ] le? def.
[ < a a > over. over. gt?. rot. rot. eq?. or. ] ge? def.
[ eq?. not. ] neq? def.
[ le?. ] ≤ def.
[ ge?. ] ≥ def.
[ neq?. ] ≠ def.

-- Stack words. `=` is `dup` and `~` is `swap` (march7/docs/SURFACE.md,
-- on trial since 2026-10-03); doubled, they act on two values at a time,
-- as FORTH's 2DUP and 2SWAP (Thomas, 2026-10-08).

[ dup. ] = def.
[ swap. ] ~ def.
[ over. over. ] dup2 def.
[ dup2. ] == def.
[ swap2. ] ~~ def.

-- Signs, as guards, and what they choose between. These clauses have no
-- `if`: a guard picks the clause (doc/design/TYPES.md 2.12).

[ 0 eq?. ] zero? def.
[ 0 gt?. ] positive? def.
[ 0 lt?. ] negative? def.

[ < a negative? -- a > -. ] abs def.
[ < a -- a > ] abs def.
[ < a a lt? -- a > drop. ] min def.
[ < a a -- a > swap. drop. ] min def.
[ < a a gt? -- a > drop. ] max def.
[ < a a -- a > swap. drop. ] max def.

[ < bool -- bool > false. eq?. ] not def.
[ < bool bool -- bool > min. ] and def.
[ < bool bool -- bool > max. ] or def.

-- Narrowing: a float to an integer, saying how it rounds: down, up, half
-- away from zero, or toward zero. A float with no i64 is an error. On an
-- integer they change nothing, and on a decimal literal they are exact.

[ < dec# -- int# > dec#-floor. ] floor def.
[ < int# -- int# > ] floor def.
[ < i64 -- i64 > ] floor def.
[ < f64 -- i64 > f64-floor. ] floor def.
[ < dec# -- int# > dec#-ceil. ] ceil def.
[ < int# -- int# > ] ceil def.
[ < i64 -- i64 > ] ceil def.
[ < f64 -- i64 > f64-ceil. ] ceil def.
[ < dec# -- int# > dec#-round. ] round def.
[ < int# -- int# > ] round def.
[ < i64 -- i64 > ] round def.
[ < f64 -- i64 > f64-round. ] round def.
[ < dec# -- int# > dec#-trunc. ] trunc def.
[ < int# -- int# > ] trunc def.
[ < i64 -- i64 > ] trunc def.
[ < f64 -- i64 > f64-trunc. ] trunc def.

-- Roots and powers. `pow` is x to the y, written `^` as LaTeX writes it
-- (Thomas, 2026-10-09); `exp`, e to the x, is kept for that. Two literals
-- stay an exact literal.

[ < f64 -- f64 > f64-sqrt. ] sqrt def.
[ < int# int# -- int# > int#-pow. ] pow def.
[ < i64 i64 -- i64 > i64-pow. ] pow def.
[ < f64 f64 -- f64 > f64-pow. ] pow def.
[ pow. ] ^ def.

-- Containers, typed by their structure. A vec knows its length at compile
-- time, so its length is a constant.

[ < string -- i64 > string-length. ] length def.
[ < a ary -- i64 > ary-length. ] length def.
[ < n a vec -- i64 > vec-length. ] length def.
[ < tuple -- i64 > tuple-length. ] length def.
[ < k v map -- i64 > map-length. ] length def.

[ < string i64 -- i64 > string-at. ] at def.
[ < a ary i64 -- a > ary-at. ] at def.
[ < n a vec i64 -- a > vec-at. ] at def.
[ < k v map k -- v > map-at. ] at def.
[ < tuple i64 > tuple-at. ] at def.
-- At an array of positions, an array: the element at each, as APL indexes.
[ < a ary i64 ary > [ over. swap. at. ] map. swap. drop. ] at def.

-- A map's value at a key, or nil where it has none: `v nil or`, decided at
-- run time unless the map and key are known (TYPES.md 3.6). Its outputs
-- name the union, so it is meant and not warned of.
[ < k v map k map-has? -- v nil or > map-at. ] get def.
[ < k v map k -- v nil or > drop. drop. nil. ] get def.

-- A map with a key set to a value: a new version, the old one unchanged.
[ < k v map k v -- k v map > map-put. ] put def.

[ < string string -- string > string-concat. ] concat def.
[ < a ary a ary -- a ary > ary-concat. ] concat def.
[ < n a vec m a vec > vec-concat. ] concat def.

[ < k v map -- k ary > map-keys. ] keys def.
[ < k v map -- v ary > map-values. ] values def.

-- `sort`: an array in order, numbers by value and strings by text.
[ < i64 ary -- i64 ary > i64-sort. ] sort def.
[ < f64 ary -- f64 ary > f64-sort. ] sort def.
[ < money ary -- money ary > money-sort. ] sort def.
[ < string ary -- string ary > string-sort. ] sort def.

-- `order`, `<#`: the positions that would put an array in order, APL's
-- grade up `⍋` and R's `order` (Thomas, 2026-10-09: not "grade", too near
-- "gradient"), so that `xs dup. order. at.` is `xs sort.`; stable, equal
-- elements keeping their order. With `at` taking an array of positions,
-- `sort-by` sorts by any key: the keys made, ordered, and the array read in
-- their order (five words, long enough to keep). `dorder`, `#>`, is the
-- descending order, as APL's `⍒`, also stable, which `order reverse` is not:
-- that would turn equal elements around. The point of `<#` and `#>` is the
-- end where the smallest go.
[ < i64 ary -- i64 ary > i64-order. ] order def.
[ < f64 ary -- i64 ary > f64-order. ] order def.
[ < money ary -- i64 ary > money-order. ] order def.
[ < string ary -- i64 ary > string-order. ] order def.
[ order. ] <# def.
[ over. swap. map. order. at. ] sort-by def.

[ < i64 ary -- i64 ary > i64-dorder. ] dorder def.
[ < f64 ary -- i64 ary > f64-dorder. ] dorder def.
[ < money ary -- i64 ary > money-dorder. ] dorder def.
[ < string ary -- i64 ary > string-dorder. ] dorder def.
[ dorder. ] #> def.

-- `take`: an array's or a string's first n elements, or its last n when n is
-- negative; all of them, if it has fewer (from SYMBOL's `↑`).
[ < a ary string or i64 negative? > -. over. length. min. over. length. dup. rot. -+. 1 +. swap. slice. ]
  take def.
[ < a ary string or i64 > over. length. min. 1 swap. slice. ] take def.

-- `skip`: all but an array's or a string's first n elements, or all but its
-- last n when n is negative; none, if it has fewer (SYMBOL's `↓`; `drop` is
-- the stack's).
[ < a ary string or i64 negative? > -. over. length. min. over. length. swap. -+. 1 swap. slice. ]
  skip def.
[ < a ary string or i64 > over. length. min. 1 +. over. length. slice. ] skip def.

-- Files (Thomas, 2026-10-09). `file` applied to a string makes a file, a
-- path said to be one, `"data.csv" file.`, so text is never taken for a
-- path. `read` reads its text when the program runs, an effect; `embed`
-- reads it while compiling, so that what follows can fold on it, as a macro
-- would, the text going into the code as data. Paths are from where march8
-- runs.
[ < string -- file > string>file. ] file def.
[ < file -- string > file-read. ] read def.

-- Text: `split` at each separator (between characters, for ""), `lines`,
-- and `words` at runs of white space, as Haskell's are; `lower` and `upper`,
-- element by element over an array of strings.
[ < string string -- string ary > string-split. ] split def.
[ < string -- string ary > string-lines. ] lines def.
[ < string -- string ary > string-words. ] words def.
[ < string -- string > string-lower. ] lower def.
[ < string -- string > string-upper. ] upper def.
[ < string ary > [ lower. ] map. ] lower def.
[ < string ary > [ upper. ] map. ] upper def.

-- `thru`: the integers from a to b, both included, counting down when a is
-- the larger (SYMBOL's `..`); `‥`, two dots as one character, is its glyph.
[ < i64 i64 gt? > over. swap. -+. 1 +. range. -. swap. 1 +. +. ] thru def.
[ < i64 i64 > over. -+. 1 +. range. swap. 1 -+. +. ] thru def.
[ thru. ] ‥ def.

-- Sums and products: `∑` and `∏` (`\sum`, `\prod`), the mathematical
-- operators, not the Greek letters (SURFACE.md). An empty array's sum is 0
-- and its product 1; a mask's sum counts its truths.
[ 0 [ +. ] fold. ] sum def.
[ 1 [ *. ] fold. ] prod def.
[ sum. ] ∑ def.
[ prod. ] ∏ def.

-- `keep`: the elements whose mask is true, APL's compress.
[ < a ary bool ary -- a ary > ary-keep. ] keep def.

-- `count` is `length`, for now (Thomas, 2026-10-08: `#` may come later).
[ length. ] count def.

[ < string i64 i64 -- string > string-slice. ] slice def.
[ < a ary i64 i64 -- a ary > ary-slice. ] slice def.

-- Elements count from 1, and from -1 at the end; gaps between them count
-- from 0, before the first, each gap after its element, so -1 is after the
-- last (Thomas, 2026-10-09). `slice` includes both its ends.

[ < a ary -- a > 1 at. ] first def.
[ < a ary -- a > -1 at. ] last def.
[ < a ary -- a ary > 2 -1 slice. ] rest def.
[ < a ary -- a ary > 1 -2 slice. ] most def.

-- `xs v k insert` puts v in the gap after element k: 0 prepends and -1
-- appends. `xs k remove` takes element k out.

[ ary-insert. ] insert def.
[ ary-remove. ] remove def.

-- Loops. `each` applies a quotation to each element, from the first, and
-- `each-right` from the last; the quotation takes its element and keeps the
-- values below it, as an accumulator. A fold starts from a value: `fold`
-- combines from the left, ((x e0) e1) e2, and `fold-right` from the right,
-- e0 (e1 (e2 x)), its quotation taking an element under the accumulator.
-- A reduction starts from the first element, or the last.

[ rot. swap. each. ] fold def.
[ rot. swap. [ swap. ] swap. compose. each-right. ] fold-right def.
[ swap. dup. rest. swap. first. rot. fold. ] reduce def.
[ swap. dup. most. swap. last. rot. fold-right. ] reduce-right def.

-- `xs q scan`: the running reductions, from the left, as `reduce` gives
-- the last of them: ( 1 2 3 ) + scan is ( 1 3 6 ). A comprehension: each
-- turn reads the last value collected.

[ ( _. dup. first. swap. rest. [ over. swap. ] _. compose. each. ) ] scan def.

-- `xs q within`: q run with the array's last elements as its stack, as many
-- as it takes, and what it leaves put in their place: `( 1 2 3 ) [ ~. ]
-- within.` is `( 1 3 2 )`. `spread` puts an array's elements on the stack:
-- a vec's each a value, and any array's, inside an array literal, a run.

[ < n a vec > vec-spread. ] spread def.
[ < tuple > tuple-spread. ] spread def.
[ < a ary > [ ] each. ] spread def.

-- `n q repeat` runs q n times.

[ swap. range. swap. [ drop. ] swap. compose. each. ] repeat def.

-- Values as text. `show` writes a value as the display does, so it reads
-- back: a string quoted, an array in parentheses. `>string` is a string's
-- own text, and any other value shown; a string's holes write values in
-- with it (march7/docs/STRINGS.md).

[ < string -- string > string-show. ] show def.
[ < i64 -- string > i64>text. ] show def.
[ < f64 -- string > f64>text. ] show def.
[ < money -- string > money>text. ] show def.
[ < nil -- string > drop. "nil" ] show def.
[ < file -- string > file-path. string-show. " file" concat. ] show def.
[ < 1 bool. -- string > drop. "true" ] show def.
[ < bool -- string > drop. "false" ] show def.
[ < tuple -- string > [ show. ] map. "(" swap. [ " " swap. concat. concat. ] each. " )" concat. ]
  show def.
[ < a ary -- string > "(" swap. [ show. " " swap. concat. concat. ] each. " )" concat. ] show def.
[ < k v map -- string >
  "{" swap. dup. keys. sort.
  [ over. over. at. show. swap. show. " " swap. concat. " " concat. swap. concat.
    rot. swap. concat. swap. ] each.
  drop. " }" concat. ] show def.

[ < string -- string > ] >string def.
[ < a -- string > show. ] >string def.

-- Writing.

[ < string > write. ] print def.
[ < a > >string. write. ] print def.
